.TH U8HD 1 "2026-11-18"
.SH NAME
u8hd \- hexadecimal, octal, ascii and UTF-8 Japanese dump
.SH SYNOPSIS
u8hd [\ \fB-abcehOrsuvw\fR\ ] [\ \fIfile\fR\ ]
[\ \fB+start[\-length|\-+end]\fR\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
u8hd dumps a file in the very same layout that jhd(1), the Japanese hex
dump tool, has always used, but its character column is built for UTF-8.
.PP
u8hd is based on jhd(1), with the character display component replaced to
support UTF-8. Started without
options, it prints for every line of a dump the offset in the file in
8 hexadecimal digits, 16 bytes of data in 2 hexadecimal digits apiece, and
the same data as characters to the right of that.  Where the data holds
multi byte characters, the character column shows the characters
themselves.  Data that repeats a line already dumped is skipped.
.PP
When no file is named and the standard input is a keyboard, the usage
message is printed.
.PP
jhd looked at the environment variable TTYPE to learn which Kanji code the
terminal spoke.  u8hd asks the locale instead, and does not look at TTYPE
at all.
.TP
a UTF-8 locale
When the code set of the locale that LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE or LANG names is
UTF-8, the character column shows the UTF-8 characters of the file as they
are.  A character that the end of a dump line cuts in two is drawn whole
at the end of the line it begins in, and the line below leaves as many
blanks in front of its own characters as bytes that character took; jhd
handled the trailing half of a Kanji the same way, except that a Kanji was
two bytes and a UTF-8 character is up to four.
.TP
any other locale
A locale that cannot display UTF-8, the C locale above all, leaves the
character column at ASCII and shows every byte of 0x80 or above as a dot.
\-u asks for UTF-8 whatever the locale says.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-a
Display ASCII characters only, taking that over what the locale said.
.TP
\-b
Dump the data byte by byte in octal.
.TP
\-c
Dump the characters only, 64 bytes to a line.
.TP
\-e
Read the file as EUC-JP.  A Kanji is two bytes, a half width katakana is
SS2 (0x8E) with one byte after it and JIS X 0212 is SS3 (0x8F) with two;
the characters are converted to UTF-8 for the display.
.TP
\-h
Write the help on the standard output: the usage, one line about every
option, one about the numbers an offset takes, the id string that tells
which version this is, and, last of all, the address of the page this
program lives on.  No file is read, and the exit status is 0.
.TP
\-o
Dump the data word by word in octal, in big-endian byte order, whatever the
machine u8hd runs on.
.TP
\-r
Dump the data word by word in octal, in little-endian byte order, whatever
the machine u8hd runs on.
.TP
\-s
Read the file as Shift-JIS.  A Kanji is two bytes and a half width
katakana is one; the characters are converted to UTF-8 for the display.
.TP
\-u
Display UTF-8 whatever the locale says.
.TP
\-v
Do not skip data that repeats.
.TP
\-w
Double the width of a dumped line: 32 bytes to a line instead of 16,
and 128 instead of 64 with \fB-c\fR.  The groups of eight bytes are kept
apart by a blank as before, so that the data of a line stand in four
columns, and the character column begins at column 111 -- which asks for
a terminal wider than the eighty columns that were usual when jhd was
written.  Nothing else changes: the same bytes are dumped, in the same
order, and a character that the end of a line cuts is still drawn where
its first byte stands.
.TP
\-O
Show the addresses in octal.
.PP
Options may be written behind one dash, so that \fB-eu\fR is the same as
\fB-e\ -u\fR, and of two options that speak about the same thing the one
written last wins: \fB-au\fR shows characters, \fB-ua\fR shows ASCII only.
\fB-e\fR and \fB-s\fR cannot be used together, since one byte of a file
cannot belong to two code sets at once.
.PP
Given as \fB-eu\fR, the file is read as EUC-JP and UTF-8 is written no
matter what the locale is; \fB-su\fR does the same for Shift-JIS.  With
\fB-e\fR or \fB-s\fR alone, the output still follows the locale, so in the
C locale a Kanji comes out as a pair of dots: add \fB-u\fR.
.SH SETTING DISPLAY RANGE
A start is written \[aq]+number\[aq], in decimal, and says where in the
file to begin looking.  An \[aq]x\[aq] before the number makes it
hexadecimal, and so does \[aq]0x\[aq] in front of it; an \[aq]o\[aq]
makes it octal, and so does a leading \[aq]0\[aq].  A \[aq]k\[aq] behind
the number multiplies it by 1024.  The offset may stand before or after the
file name.
.PP
A dash behind the offset narrows the dump to a range, and what follows the
dash says how.  A plain number counts the bytes to show; a number with an
\[aq]+\[aq] in front is the last byte to show, that byte still being a part
of the dump.  The beginning of the range may be left out, which is the
beginning of the file.  Both of its numbers take the \[aq]x\[aq], the
\[aq]o\[aq] and the \[aq]k\[aq] of the offset.
.in +4n
.nf
+1000\-20        twenty bytes, beginning at byte 1000
+1000\-+1019     the same twenty, told by their places
+\-+511          the first 512 bytes of the file
+0x40\-0x20      the 32 bytes from 0x40 to 0x5f
+1k\-512         the 512 bytes behind the first kilobyte
.fi
.in -4n
.SH CODE SET CONVERSION
libiconv(3) does the conversion of EUC-JP and Shift-JIS into UTF-8.  A
file that already is UTF-8 needs none, and no iconv descriptor is opened
for it.  Bytes that make no character, and characters that cannot be
converted, are shown as a dot.
.SH EXAMPLE
.in +4n
.nf
$ cat hello
Hello ¡Hola! こんにちは 안녕하세요 你好
$ u8hd hello
00000000  48 65 6C 6C 6F 20 C2 A1  48 6F 6C 61 21 20 E3 81  Hello ¡Hola! こ
00000010  93 E3 82 93 E3 81 AB E3  81 A1 E3 81 AF 20 EC 95   んにちは 안
00000020  88 EB 85 95 ED 95 98 EC  84 B8 EC 9A 94 20 E4 BD   녕하세요 你
00000030  A0 E5 A5 BD 0A                                     好.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 4n
0
The dump was written.
.TP 4n
1
Bad usage, a file that cannot be opened, an offset behind the data, an end
behind its start, or an iconv that does not know the conversion.
.SH SEE ALSO
od(1), jhd(1), iconv(3), nl_langinfo(3), setlocale(3)
.SH AUTHOR
Masato Minda <minmin@wide.ad.jp>
.PP
u8hd is the layout of jhd(1) written out for UTF-8.  Where jhd chooses its
Kanji code when it is compiled, EUC-JP or Shift-JIS, u8hd chooses it on the
command line with -e and -s.  A Japanese manual page comes with the
distribution as u8hd_ja.1.
